#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133698 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133882 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "bf385144-2f48-5874-8c0d-dec31d8fc6e3" :knowledge-point/title "Expressing a Line Integral as a Double Integral Using Green's Theorem"}] :question/id #uuid "69c1029c-777f-5a16-8ff5-9f8f0c9a48b2" :question/math-academy-id "e-9071" :question/problem "Consider the triangle $C$ in the plane with vertices $(0,0), (1,0)$ and $(0,2),$ and the region $D$ bounded by $C.$ According to Green's theorem, \n\n$$\n\\displaystyle{\\oint\\limits_{C}\\cos{y}\\,\\mathrm{d}x+x\\sin{y}\\,\\mathrm{d}y}= \\iint\\limits_D f(x,y) \\,\\textrm d A,\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is traversed counterclockwise. Calculate the function $f(x,y).$" :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first partial derivatives on an open region containing $D$, then Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n\\oint\\limits_{C} P\\,\\mathrm{d}x + Q\\,\\mathrm{d}y \n= \\iint\\limits_D \\left(\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x} - \\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}\\right)\\,\\textrm d A.\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and the curve $C$ for this case are shown below.\n\n![](images/a7/a78a98750e3203760d27daaebd42852198538935e2c3777a0b8a8c85d1bb0901.png)\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)= \\cos{y}$ and $Q(x,y)= x\\sin{y}.$ So,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡y,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y\\,dx+x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡y-(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}2\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $f(x,y) = 2\\sin{y}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133699 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133883 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "11d3f9e1-dad0-51d4-86a9-e0c49465cc3c" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating Line Integrals Using Green's Theorem"}] :question/id #uuid "2109910f-fc87-5e56-b2f6-12132b9f6073" :question/math-academy-id "e-9072" :question/problem "Use Green's theorem to evaluate $\\displaystyle{\\oint\\limits_{C} 4x^2y \\, \\mathrm{d}x+ 2y^2\\,\\mathrm{d}y},$ where $C$ is the boundary of the triangle with vertices at $(0,0),$ $(1,2)$ and $(0,2),$ traversed counterclockwise." :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first partial derivatives on an open region containing $D$, then Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n\\oint\\limits_{C} P\\,\\mathrm{d}x + Q\\,\\mathrm{d}y \n= \\iint\\limits_D \\left(\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x} - \\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}\\right)\\textrm d A.\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and the curve $C$ for this case are shown below.\n\n \n\n![](images/a8/a87f696b1eb42588ab69800b72d821bf47b35676fc19dcd4fe2927bbf36ba3f8.png)\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)= 4x^2y$ and $Q(x,y)= 2y^2.$ So,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=4{x}^{2},\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can express the region $D$ as a type I region as follows:\n\n$$\nD = \\left\\{(x,y) : 0 \\leq x \\leq 1,\\, 2x \\leq y \\leq 2 \\right\\}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's Theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}4{x}^{2}y\\,dx+2{y}^{2}\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(0-4{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{2x}^{2}(-4{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-4{\\int }_{0}^{1}{x}^{2}[y]_{2x}^{2}\\,dx \\\\  & =-4{\\int }_{0}^{1}{x}^{2}(2-2x)\\,dx \\\\  & =-8{\\int }_{0}^{1}{x}^{2}(1-x)\\,dx \\\\  & =-8{\\int }_{0}^{1}{x}^{2}-{x}^{3}\\,dx \\\\  & =-8{[\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}-\\frac{{x}^{4}}{4}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =-8(\\frac{1}{3}-\\frac{1}{4}) \\\\  & =-8\\cdot \\frac{1}{12} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133700 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133884 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "48652211-e469-55b9-96cb-2526a4135d34" :knowledge-point/title "Expressing a Line Integral as a Repeated Integral Using Green's Theorem"}] :question/id #uuid "3ff94d55-a99a-5de6-bc84-410d03f18a28" :question/math-academy-id "e-9086" :question/problem "Consider the triangle $C$ in the plane with vertices $(0,0), (1,1)$ and $(-1,1),$ traversed counterclockwise, and the region $D$ bounded by $C.$ Express $\\displaystyle{\\oint\\limits_{C}x\\sin{y}\\,\\mathrm{d}x+ (x^2\\cos{y}-y^2)\\,\\mathrm{d}y}$ as a repeated integral." :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first partial derivatives on an open region containing $D$, then Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n\\oint\\limits_{C} P\\,\\mathrm{d}x + Q\\,\\mathrm{d}y \n= \\iint\\limits_D \\left(\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x} - \\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}\\right)\\,\\textrm d A.\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and the curve $C$ for this case are shown below.\n\n![](images/2f/2fa3c84359cec5f5e0750552e5a4b24b20b8dbc3d65b909aea908e1505cfb5e3.png)\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)= x\\sin{y}$ and $Q(x,y)= x^2\\cos{y}-y^2.$ So,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=2x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can express the region $D$ as a type II region as follows:\n\n$$\nD = \\{(x,y) :  -y\\leq x\\leq y,\\, 0\\leq y \\leq 1\\}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's Theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\,dx+({x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y-{y}^{2})\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(2x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y-x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{-y}^{y}x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134367 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133883 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "11d3f9e1-dad0-51d4-86a9-e0c49465cc3c" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating Line Integrals Using Green's Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a1c3594e-1c5d-5b05-b194-54b49edf7606" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "π(π^{2} - 2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186132242} {:answer/id #uuid "b79e08a7-9ea2-5371-a822-8e10b12b596c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "π(π^{2} + 2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186132243} {:answer/id #uuid "012f99f7-dcbe-51fa-83a8-205ce93d2f30" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "π^{2} + 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186132244} {:answer/id #uuid "41f25afb-5325-5f8a-9e43-e0e0e1f00768" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2π^{2} - 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186132245} {:answer/id #uuid "57e71d2e-b1f6-5aa6-bb9f-240cfb7405c5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "π^{2} - 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186132246}] :answer-field/id #uuid "9e8398a8-2f78-58ed-934c-e94f4a278f78" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186132241}] :question/id #uuid "4381a978-8315-576b-b33a-7ce6b2ca6e4b" :question/math-academy-id "q-41810" :question/problem "Use Green's theorem to evaluate $∮_(C)F \\cdot dr$, where $F(x, y) = ⟨\\cos y, x^{2} - 2y⟩$ and $C$ is the boundary of the square $0 \\le x \\le π, 0 \\le y \\le π$, traversed counterclockwise."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134375 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133884 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "48652211-e469-55b9-96cb-2526a4135d34" :knowledge-point/title "Expressing a Line Integral as a Repeated Integral Using Green's Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8617da99-e68c-59ad-82d2-8e06c3d7681d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\displaystyle \\int_{-1}^{3}\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{2}y(2x - 3)dydx" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186132290} {:answer/id #uuid "937a6444-c95c-5b18-aa51-9175a15b8661" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\displaystyle \\int_{-1}^{2}\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{3}x(3y + 2)dydx" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186132291} {:answer/id #uuid "9d48eea4-f9b1-52d9-ac1e-81e28666d349" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\displaystyle \\int_{-1}^{2}\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{3}(x^{2} - 3y)dydx" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186132292} {:answer/id #uuid "ca0f599d-b134-5867-9267-1f45a84dfbe7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\displaystyle \\int_{-1}^{2}\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{3}x(2y - 3)dydx" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186132293} {:answer/id #uuid "d91cc82b-33c9-5e2e-af2e-195acfb0b102" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\displaystyle \\int_{-1}^{3}\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{2}x(3y - 2)dydx" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186132294}] :answer-field/id #uuid "2dab8859-4468-5634-8462-ee68c975e5ce" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186132289}] :question/id #uuid "1939a88a-677c-5293-8524-d0ecfaa79350" :question/math-academy-id "q-42085" :question/problem "Consider the square $C$ in the plane with vertices $(-1, 0), (2, 0), (2, 3)$ and $(-1, 3)$, traversed counterclockwise, and the region $D$ bounded by $C$. Express $∮_(C)F \\cdot dr$, where $F(x, y) = ⟨3xy, x^{2}y⟩$, as a repeated integral."}]}
